$DATUM 7789-45-9
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):SOL(1):CAS_RN
$DATUM 75-05-8
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: To a mixture of copper source (1.2 eq., CuBr2
for 4b, CuCl2 for 4c, CuI for 4d) in acetonitrile (0.4M) was slowly added
tBuONO (1.5 eq.). The mixture was stirred for 15 min and then cooled to
0°C. A solution of 2-chloro-4-aminopyridine 3 (1.0 eq.) in acetonitrile
(0.5M) was slowly added. The mixture was stirred for 1h at 0°C and then
allowed to warm to r.t. for 16h. After concentration in vacuo, aq. 15%
ammonia solution (1.3 mL/mmol) was added, and the aqueous layer was
extracted with dichloromethane (10 mL/mmol; 3x). The combined organic
layers were washed with brine, dried (Na2SO4), filtered and carefully
concentrated under reduced pressure (CAUTION: volatile products) to give
the crude 4b-d, which was used in the next step without purification.
4-Chloro-3-chloropyridine (4b) 4-Bromo-3-chloropyridine (4c) [3] Carried
out on 60.0 mmol scale; yellow liquid (9.21 g, 96%). 1H NMR (400 MHz,
CDCl3,) δ 8.23 (dd, 1H, J = 5.4 Hz, J = 0.5 Hz), 7.54 (dd, 1H, J = 1.60
Hz, J = 0.5 Hz), 7.40 (dd, 1H, J = 5.4 Hz, J = 1.6 Hz); 13C NMR (100 MHz,
CDCl3) δ 152.2, 150.1, 134.2, 127.3, 125.9.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM regioselective, safety, Sandmeyer reaction, volatile product
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Concise Entries to 4-Halo-2-pyridones and 3-Bromo-4-halo-2-pyridones
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Honraedt, Aurelien; Gallagher, Timothy
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Synlett (2016), 27(1), 67-69

